title: The policy that was attached, visible, and inert
subtitle: One word changed. Nothing else. The policy document is identical.
width: 1120
---
WITH auth_type = AWS_IAM                    WITH auth_type = NONE

$ aws vpc-lattice get-auth-policy --resource-identifier <service-arn>

  state: Active                      [ok]     state: Inactive         [FAIL]

  policy: {                                   policy: {
    "Sid": "OnlyTheOrdersClient...              "Sid": "OnlyTheOrdersClient...
    "Principal": {                              "Principal": {
      "AWS": ".../orders-client"                  "AWS": ".../orders-client"
    },                                          },
    "Condition": {                              "Condition": {
      "RequestMethod": "GET"                      "RequestMethod": "GET"
    }                                           }
  }                                           }

                         IDENTICAL DOCUMENT

--- the intruder, wrong role AND wrong method -----------------------------

$ aws lambda invoke --function-name week23-lattice-intruder \
      --payload '{"mode":"signed","method":"POST","path":"/rates"}'

  status: 200                                                       [FAIL]
  {"service": "rates", "method": "POST", "rate": 42.5}

The wrong identity, using the wrong method, served happily. The policy
naming one role and one method is still attached and still visible in the
console.

--- but the system still looks healthy ------------------------------------

$ ... --payload '{"mode":"unsigned","method":"POST","path":"/rates"}'

  status: 403
  User: anonymous ... no NETWORK-BASED policy allows this action

The SERVICE policy went silent. The SERVICE NETWORK policy did not. So the
system kept refusing anonymous callers the whole time.

Spot-check from outside the VPC and you get a 403 and conclude the controls
are working. A half-disabled two-layer control looks healthier than a fully
disabled one, because something is still saying no.
